---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const BASE_URL = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="Research Radar">
  <main class="project">
    <p class="back-top"><a href={BASE_URL + 'projects'}>← All projects</a></p>
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>AI Research Radar</h1>
      <p class="tagline">A weekly agent that monitors new AI governance research and produces a consultant-ready digest.</p>
      <p class="meta">Python · OpenAI · OpenAlex · GitHub Actions · 2026</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar/learnings'}>Learnings</a>
        <a class="btn" href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar/design-decisions'}>Design decisions</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/alawal1/research-radar" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a>
        <a class="btn" href={BASE_URL + 'projects/research-radar/updates'}>Updates</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Staying current on AI governance research is slow and manual. Papers are scattered
        across arXiv, SSRN, Zenodo, and dozens of journals. A consultant tracking EU AI Act
        compliance, auditing standards, or AI liability has to search the same databases
        every week, read abstracts to judge relevance, and separate serious research from
        opinion pieces and academic tangents.
      </p>
      <p>
        This takes hours. Most people give up and miss the papers that matter.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Searches OpenAlex every Monday for AI governance papers published in the last 7 days</li>
        <li>Filters each paper for relevance (Gate 1) and quality (Gate 2)</li>
        <li>Scores papers on four feasibility criteria</li>
        <li>Writes a digest covering everything that passed</li>
        <li>Commits the digest to the repo</li>
        <li>Saves the top 5 papers as a CSV for further analysis</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The agent groups 15 search keywords into 2–4 queries and runs them one at a time.
        After each query, the LLM decides whether to keep searching or stop. Papers that
        pass both gates are scored on four criteria — relevant, positioned against prior
        work, practical, rigorous. The digest covers all of them. The top 5 by score are
        saved as a CSV.
      </p>
      <p>
        Facts come from the API; judgments come from the LLM. The agent never lets the LLM
        touch metadata, and never lets the API judge quality. They meet only when a row is
        written.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={BASE_URL + 'projects'}>← All projects</a></p>
  </main>

  <style>
    .project { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.65; }
    .hero { text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0 3rem; }
    .hero h1 { font-size: 2.5rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
    .tagline { font-size: 1.15rem; opacity: 0.8; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
    .meta { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 0 0 1.75rem; }
    .actions { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .btn {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;
      padding: 0.55rem 1.1rem; border-radius: 999px;
      background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
      text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 500;
      transition: background 0.15s ease;
    }
    .btn:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18); }
    .releases-link { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; }
    section { margin: 3rem 0; }
    section h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
    .back { margin-top: 4rem; opacity: 0.7; }
    .back-top { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 2rem 0 0; }
    .back-top a { text-decoration: none; }
    .actions { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  </style>
</Layout>
